title: Tutorial — How This Works
authors: Writing Journals tutorial, built in lo-blocks
description: Professor Ellington spends two minutes showing the student how the tool works — the conversation advances on Continue, activities land in the right pane, work saves as you type, and the tabs collect everything — then sends them to the Writers' Circle for their first journal.
~~~~

// HOW THE ACTIVITY PANE IS DRIVEN.
//
// Every card the conversation opens on the right is written as a TARGET EMBED:
//
//     ::tut_welcome [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Welcome"]
//
// which repoints the tut_sidebar UseHistory at that screen (once, the first
// time the line is revealed) AND leaves a small labelled button in the
// transcript at the point in the conversation where the card arrived. Clicking
// that button repoints the pane there again.
//
// In this tutorial that marker is not just plumbing — it is the lesson of the
// "Looking Back" section: the way back to an earlier activity is to find the
// moment in the conversation and click its button. The UseHistory arrows at the
// top of the pane still do the same job and get a one-line mention.
//
// Timing note: a set command executes while the reveal walks past it; an embed
// is content, so it is revealed as its own beat — the card lands one Continue
// BEFORE the line that talks about it, which is the order you want. Ellington
// glosses the button the first time one appears so it isn't unexplained noise.
//
// The Flash/FocusAction Triggers in tutorial_sba.olx watch @tut_sidebar.value
// and are unaffected: an embed writes the same field with the same values the
// arrows did — including on a marker click, so a revisit pulses too.

How This Works
--------------
Ellington: before I hand you over to your first online journal, give me two minutes to show you how this thing works. [face=calm]

Ellington: this left side is a conversation. it moves when you click **Continue** below — which you've apparently already worked out. [face=smile]

::tut_welcome [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Welcome"]

Ellington: the right side is where the work happens. read the card that just landed, then click **Mark as read** at the bottom of it — **Continue** stays off until you do. it's over there ---> [face=explaining]

Ellington: (that little **Welcome** button that just appeared here in the chat is a bookmark to it. ignore it for now — I'll come back to it.) [face=calm]

--- wait @tut_welcome_done.value ---

Try It Once
-----------
::tut_practice [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Try it"]

Ellington: good. now the other half — when there's something to *do*, it shows up the same way, and it pulses to catch your eye. type anything at all in the box; one word is plenty. [face=explaining]

--- wait @tut_practice_text.value ---

Ellington: notice you never clicked "save" — there is no save button anywhere in here. every keystroke is kept as you go. you could close this tab mid-sentence and your half-sentence would be waiting when you came back. [face=calm]

Looking Back
------------
// Taught by doing, like everything else here. Every card the chat opens is a
// target embed, so it leaves a labelled button in the transcript at the moment
// it arrived — that button is the thing being taught, because it is the one way
// back that also tells you WHERE in the conversation the card belonged.
// The gate is unchanged from the arrow version: clicking the **Welcome** marker
// writes tut_sidebar the same way a ‹ click does, so either route opens it.
// The UseHistory bar (‹ arrow, one dot per card seen, › arrow) is kept as a
// one-line aside rather than a second exercise — one move per beat.
Ellington: one more move, and it's the one people miss. remember that bookmark button? every card that lands on the right leaves one, right where it came up in our conversation. [face=explaining]

Ellington: scroll our chat back up and click the **Welcome** one — the moment the first card arrived. [face=explaining]

--- wait @tut_sidebar.value === 'tut_welcome' ---

Ellington: there it is, just as you left it. that's the way back to anything: find the moment in the conversation, click its button, and the card returns. looking back never undoes anything. [face=smile]

Ellington: (the ‹ › arrows at the very top of the right side walk the same history, if you'd rather step through it.) click **Continue** and I'll bring you back to where we were. [face=calm]

The Tabs
--------
::tut_tabs_note [display=target:tut_sidebar label="The tabs"]

Ellington: last thing. the tabs along the top collect everything in one place — in the journals that's your running work, the people you're talking to, and the page where you export a PDF to hand in at the end. [face=explaining]

Ellington: the conversation will point you to a tab whenever it matters, and you can always come back — nothing locks behind you. try the **Tips** tab up top if you're curious, then come back to **Main Content**. [face=smile]

That's Everything
-----------------
::tut_finish [display=target:tut_sidebar label="That's everything"]

Ellington: that's the whole machine. conversation on the left, work on the right, tabs up top. [face=smile]

Ellington: you did your first writing journal on paper. from here they move online — the next one is called "Who Am I As a Writer?", and three students who call themselves the Writers' Circle are expecting you. tap **Continue** and I'll take you over. [face=calm]

--- pause ---

wj_course.selectedChild <- edu.memphis.writing.sba/wj1_sba_part1
